Obviously I enjoy Crawl or I wouldn't play it. :) I wouldn't really
recommend it to people who hadn't ascended NetHack or some other roguelike
at least a couple of times, though; it's really unrelentingly harsh.
When I play a game of NetHack, I expect to win unless I mess it up;
maybe I just haven't reached that point with Crawl yet, but it seems like
there are a lot of unavoidable or only marginally unavoidable deaths well
into the game. There also isn't a whole lot of potential for your early
game to really break open, at least with most classes; NetHack has the
fountain dip and the elusive hope of the early wand of wishing, while
the best you could do in early Crawl is more character-class dependent
and much less unbalancing.

I think that's OK and even good, and in fact it's why I like to play
Crawl, but I think if you're used to NetHack it's a real shock. Also,
for some of the readers here, I think my statement was actually a good
advertisement; it was comments like that here that first got me paying
attention to Crawl. "Everyone thinks Crawl is so hard? Pish tosh! I
can ascend five NetHack games in a row, clearly this is trivia---OH GOD
I DIED." :)

I certainly don't mean to harsh on Crawl; I have NetHack moods and Crawl
moods, and I've been in a NetHack mood more recently, but in a lot of
ways I think Crawl is actually the better dungeon crawler; NetHack is
just the best NetHack.

